WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.650
You open your laptop to finish one thing,
then a message

2
00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:05.500
appears.

3
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.000
You close it.

4
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
A tab reminds you of something else.

5
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.500
You check your phone without even knowing
why.

6
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.740
30 seconds later, the document is still
there, but the thought

7
00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.500
you had is gone.

8
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.000
Maybe your attention span isn't broken.

9
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Maybe your task just has too many exits.

10
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:33.250
And if that's the problem, forcing
yourself to focus harder may

11
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:34.500
be the wrong fix.

12
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.970
You may have heard that the average
attention span is now just

13
00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.500
47 seconds.

14
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.500
That number is real, but it is often
explained incorrectly.

15
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:49.390
Researchers observed people using
computers in their normal

16
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.500
working environments.

17
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:55.220
In recent studies, people spent an average
of about 47 seconds

18
00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.500
on one screen before switching to another.

19
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:03.500
Not one idea. Not one project. One screen.

20
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:06.810
Opening a reference for the document you
are writing counts as

21
00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:07.500
a switch.

22
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.820
So does checking a message, looking at the
clock, or opening a

23
00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.470
new tab because you suddenly need to know
how many volcanoes

24
00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:16.500
Iceland has.

25
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.700
47 seconds is not a biological countdown,
after which your

26
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.500
brain becomes incapable of attention.

27
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.650
It is an observation of digital behavior
in environments filled

28
00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:29.500
with possible switches.

29
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:32.500
And averages hide variation.

30
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:35.500
Sometimes we stay with one screen for
several minutes.

31
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.500
Sometimes we leave almost immediately.

32
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.730
So the useful question is not, "How do I
force my brain to

33
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:44.500
focus forever?"

34
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.500
It is, "What keeps making this task so
easy to leave?"

35
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.500
Some exits are external.

36
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.500
A notification appears.

37
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.500
Someone sends a message.

38
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.500
A browser badge turns orange.

39
00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.500
A coworker asks a question.

40
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.700
These interruptions are easy to blame
because we can see them

41
00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:06.500
arrive.

42
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:09.500
But many exits are self-created.

43
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.500
You wonder whether someone replied. So you
check.

44
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.500
The current paragraph becomes difficult.

45
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:17.500
So you open another tab.

46
00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.480
You remember a small, unrelated task and
decide to do it now

47
00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.500
before you forget.

48
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.500
Nothing interrupted you.

49
00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:29.500
You interrupted yourself.

50
00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:33.450
This explains why turning off
notifications can help without

51
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.500
solving the entire problem.

52
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.600
In one randomized intervention,
participants disabled

53
00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.500
smartphone notifications for a week.

54
00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.690
But this did not significantly reduce how
often they checked

55
00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.500
their phones or their total screen time.

56
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.500
Remove the alert and the checking habit
can remain.

57
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.560
At the same time, we should not pretend
that every notification

58
00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.500
destroys 20 minutes of productivity.

59
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.900
In one laboratory task, smartphone
notification sounds slowed

60
00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:06.500
responses by only a few milliseconds on
average.

61
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.500
The measured effect was small.

62
00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:13.500
One sound is not a cognitive meteor
strike.

63
00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.500
The larger problem is the system around
it.

64
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:20.970
Dozens of possible destinations, familiar
checking habits, and

65
00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:25.500
difficult moments that make another screen
feel more attractive.

66
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.500
An exit does not have to drag you away.

67
00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.500
It only has to make leaving easier than
continuing.

68
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.500
Imagine that you are assembling a small
model in your head.

69
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.530
You know what the paragraph is trying to
say, which sentence

70
00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:43.500
feels wrong, and what should come next.

71
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.500
Then you switch to a message.

72
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:50.500
The model may not disappear, but it
becomes harder to access.

73
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:54.500
When you return, you have to reconstruct
your position.

74
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.500
What was I doing?

75
00:03:56.500 --> 00:03:58.500
What had I already decided?

76
00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.500
What was the next move?

77
00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.740
Researchers call the delay before
continuing an interrupted

78
00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:07.500
task a resumption lag.

79
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.390
In experiments, people resumed faster when
they had a brief

80
00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:13.500
opportunity to prepare for the
interruption

81
00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:18.500
or when useful cues from the original task
remained available.

82
00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:21.480
This is why a document can be open
directly in front of you.

83
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:25.500
while your mental position inside it is
missing.

84
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.670
You may also have heard that it takes
around 25 minutes to

85
00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:30.500
recover from an interruption.

86
00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.500
That wording is misleading.

87
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.070
The field research measured how long it
took people to return

88
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:40.500
to the original project after moving
through other activities.

89
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:44.750
It does not mean every notification leaves
every person unable

90
00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.500
to focus for exactly 25 minutes.

91
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:49.500
Sometimes you return quickly.

92
00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:52.500
Sometimes one switch creates a chain.

93
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:58.290
Message, email, calendar, search, another
message, then the

94
00:04:58.290 --> 00:04:59.500
original document.

95
00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:03.390
Interrupted work can even look productive
because people

96
00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.500
compensate by working faster.

97
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:09.390
In one experiment, participants completed
interrupted tasks

98
00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:11.500
faster without a measured drop in quality

99
00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:15.580
but reported more stress, frustration,
effort, and time

100
00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:16.500
pressure.

101
00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:20.500
The cost is not always visible in the
finished document.

102
00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:23.500
Sometimes it appears in the person
finishing it.

103
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:27.390
Instead of treating attention like a
muscle that must be

104
00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:30.500
screamed at, change the architecture of
the task.

105
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.880
For one session, give the task one obvious
entrance and as few

106
00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:36.500
exits as possible.

107
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.500
This is the One Door Protocol.

108
00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:43.390
It is not a clinically validated treatment
or a magical

109
00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:44.500
productivity formula.

110
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.510
It is a practical experiment built from
what interruption

111
00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:52.850
research suggests about switching, cues,
and returning to a

112
00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:53.500
task.

113
00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.500
Step 1. Name one observable outcome.

114
00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.500
Study biology is not an observable
outcome.

115
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:05.500
Review Chapter 4 is better but still
leaves room for wandering.

116
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:10.350
Create 10 flashcards from Chapter 4 gives
the session a visible

117
00:06:10.350 --> 00:06:11.500
finish line.

118
00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:16.500
For writing, draft a 150-word opening
paragraph.

119
00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:19.500
For administration, complete and send the
form.

120
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:22.500
Describe something you can see when it is
done.

121
00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:27.500
Now you do not have to keep deciding what
"working" means.

122
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:30.500
Step 2. Close visible exits.

123
00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.500
Keep only what the outcome requires.

124
00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:35.500
Close unrelated tabs.

125
00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:36.500
Hide badges.

126
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.920
Put the phone somewhere that requires a
deliberate movement to

127
00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:40.500
reach.

128
00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:44.500
You do not have to transform your room
into a silent monastery.

129
00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:48.790
The goal is simply to add friction between
an impulse and an

130
00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:49.500
exit.

131
00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:53.630
If the phone is already in your hand,
checking it is almost

132
00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:54.500
effortless.

133
00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.390
If it is across the room, the impulse gets
a few seconds to

134
00:06:58.390 --> 00:06:59.500
become visible.

135
00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.500
That pause gives you a choice.

136
00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:07.500
Step 3. Park unrelated impulses. You will
remember things.

137
00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.500
Buy toothpaste. Reply to Alex.

138
00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:14.500
Check whether Iceland really has that many
volcanoes.

139
00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.440
Trying to not think about these things can
become another

140
00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:18.500
distraction.

141
00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.500
Acting on each thought is worse.

142
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:24.500
Keep a small parking list beside the task.

143
00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:28.290
Write two or three words, just enough to
recover the thought

144
00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:29.500
later, and continue.

145
00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.500
The list is not a second project.

146
00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.460
Do not organize it, decorate it, or turn
it into a color-coded

147
00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:38.500
productivity ecosystem.

148
00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.500
It is temporary storage for exits you
chose not to take.

149
00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.500
Step 4. Leave a return cue.

150
00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.500
Some interruptions are unavoidable. Before
leaving the task,

151
00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.500
write the next physical action.

152
00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:58.290
Not "continue report," write "compare the
final two numbers in

153
00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:02.930
Table 3," or "explain why the second
example contradicts the

154
00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:03.500
first."

155
00:08:03.500 --> 00:08:08.500
A useful return cue stores your position
outside your head.

156
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:12.520
Research on interrupted tasks suggests
that even a short

157
00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:17.500
opportunity to encode the next goal can
make resumption easier.

158
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.390
You cannot prevent every interruption, but
you can make

159
00:08:21.390 --> 00:08:23.500
returning less expensive.

160
00:08:23.500 --> 00:08:27.500
Step 5. Stop at a natural breakpoint.

161
00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:30.500
Focus is not the same thing as refusing to
stop.

162
00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:33.640
If you reach the end of a section, finish
a problem, or

163
00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.820
complete the defined outcome, that may be
a good place for a

164
00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:38.500
break.

165
00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.500
Before leaving, create the next return
cue.

166
00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.390
Now the task has a handle. When you come
back, you do not need

167
00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:47.500
to rediscover where to grab it.

168
00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:51.500
Do not rebuild your entire life around
this protocol yet.

169
00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.500
Test it once.

170
00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.640
Choose one task you have been avoiding,
and define one

171
00:08:57.640 --> 00:08:59.500
observable outcome.

172
00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:02.500
Set a timer for 25 minutes.

173
00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:07.260
Not because 25 is a magical neurological
number, but because it

174
00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.500
is long enough to observe your switching
habits,

175
00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:12.500
and short enough to attempt today.

176
00:09:12.500 --> 00:09:14.500
Close visible exits.

177
00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:16.500
Keep a parking list.

178
00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.500
Then make a mark every time you want to
leave the task.

179
00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.500
Do not judge the impulse. Just record it.

180
00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:27.500
At the end, look at the list.

181
00:09:27.500 --> 00:09:29.500
Which exits were external?

182
00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:32.500
Which ones did you create yourself?

183
00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:37.220
Which appeared when the work became
confusing, uncomfortable,

184
00:09:37.220 --> 00:09:38.500
or boring?

185
00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:42.390
The goal is not to prove that you possess
extraordinary

186
00:09:42.390 --> 00:09:43.500
discipline.

187
00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.500
It is to make the exits visible.

188
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:50.500
Because once you can see the architecture
of your attention,

189
00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:51.500
you can start changing it.

190
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:55.500
Your attention is not a purity test.

191
00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.810
And it is probably not repaired by
deleting every app, sitting

192
00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:04.500
in an empty room, or attempting a literal
dopamine detox.

193
00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.500
You do not need to become a person who
never gets distracted.

194
00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:13.550
You need a task that is easier to return
to, and an environment

195
00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:16.500
where leaving requires a conscious choice.

196
00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:21.500
Try one task, one outcome, and one door.

197
00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.500
Then notice which exit you still try to
take.

198
00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.500
Messages?

199
00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:27.500
Tabs?

200
00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.500
Your phone?

201
00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.500
Or something else?
